##############################################################################
# RC4 + Base64 PowerShell Stager/Loader Pattern - Suricata/Security Onion
#
# Target activity: a PowerShell tool that RC4-encrypts a file with a
# hardcoded key, Base64-encodes the ciphertext, and optionally remaps the
# Base64 alphabet. The author explicitly frames it as demonstrating "the
# canonical shape of a loader/stager" used in red team tradecraft.
#
# IMPORTANT SCOPE NOTE: this tool's own writeup is about file-to-file
# transformation on disk - it does not itself define a network protocol.
# The best real detections for it are HOST-based (AMSI, PowerShell Script
# Block Logging / Event ID 4104, and Sigma rules matching the RC4 KSA loop
# / 256-element state array / base64-near-XOR-loop structure the article
# describes) - those belong in Security Onion's Sigma/EDR pipeline, not
# a Suricata .rules file, and are NOT duplicated here.
#
# What IS legitimately network-visible is the delivery/staging step: this
# loader shape is normally fetched or dropped over HTTP(S) as either (a)
# the PowerShell source itself, or (b) the resulting RC4+Base64 blob being
# pulled down as stage 2. Both are covered below. Treat this file as a
# thin, low-confidence complement to the LOLBin/PowerShell rules you
# already have - not a substitute for host-side detection of this
# specific technique.
#
# SID range: 1000600-1000699 (local convention, 1,000,000+).
##############################################################################

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Detect the loader's own PowerShell SOURCE being staged/downloaded over
# HTTP. Keys on the combination of the hardcoded key literal the article
# calls out ("pass code") together with an RC4-shaped 256-element
# key-scheduling loop - reduces false positives vs. either string alone.
# High value against out-of-the-box, unmodified copies of this specific
# script; trivially defeated by renaming the key or the variable names.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note: a generic "client requested a .ps1 file" rule already lives in
# web-hosted-shell-traffic.rules (SID 1000408) - not duplicated here.
alert http any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"REDTEAM - PowerShell RC4/Base64 Loader Source Served Over HTTP (Hardcoded 'pass code' Key)"; flow:established,to_client; file_data; content:"pass code"; nocase; content:"256"; distance:0; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:1000601; rev:1; metadata:created_at 2026_08_18, updated_at 2026_08_18, tool RC4Base64Loader;)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage-2 delivery: the RC4+Base64 output blob itself. This is
# unavoidably a weak, generic heuristic - a large, unbroken run of
# standard Base64 characters with no recognizable file magic bytes is
# consistent with an encrypted/encoded stager, but is also consistent
# with a great deal of entirely legitimate web content (fonts, inline
# images, JS source maps, JWTs, etc.). Treat as low-confidence hunting
# only; pair with PowerShell UA context from your LOLBin ruleset to cut
# noise, and expect to tune the run-length threshold per environment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert http any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"REDTEAM - Large Contiguous Base64 Blob In HTTP Response To PowerShell Client (Possible Encoded Stager)"; flow:established,to_client; http.user_agent; content:"PowerShell"; file_data; pcre:"/^[A-Za-z0-9+\/]{500,}={0,2}/"; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:1000602; rev:1; metadata:created_at 2026_08_18, updated_at 2026_08_18, tool RC4Base64Loader;)

##############################################################################
# Tuning notes:
#  - This technique is fundamentally a host/scripting-level pattern. If
#    you only deploy the rules in this file you will miss most real-world
#    use of this loader shape - pair with AMSI, Script Block Logging
#    (Event ID 4104), and a Sigma rule for the RC4 KSA/PRGA + base64
#    structure the article describes.
#  - SID 1000602 is intentionally conservative (500+ contiguous base64
#    chars) but will still need allow-listing against known chatty
#    internal PowerShell automation (e.g. artifact upload scripts) before
#    running in anything but hunting mode.
##############################################################################
